247	## Why MCP?
248	
249	The Model Context Protocol (MCP) provides several key advantages for Spark code optimization:
250	
251	
252	### Direct Claude AI Call vs MCP Server
253	
254	| Aspect | Direct Claude AI Call | MCP Server |
255	|--------|-----------------|------------|
256	| **Integration** | • Custom integration per team<br>• Manual response handling<br>• Duplicate implementations | • Pre-built client libraries<br>• Automated workflows<br>• Unified interfaces |
257	| **Infrastructure** | • No built-in validation<br>• No result persistence<br>• Manual tracking | • Automatic validation<br>• Result persistence<br>• Version control |
258	| **Context** | • Basic code suggestions<br>• No execution context<br>• Limited optimization scope | • Context-aware optimization<br>• Full execution history<br>• Comprehensive improvements |
259	| **Validation** | • Manual testing required<br>• No performance metrics<br>• Uncertain outcomes | • Automated testing<br>• Performance metrics<br>• Validated results |
260	| **Workflow** | • Ad-hoc process<br>• No standardization<br>• Manual intervention needed | • Structured process<br>• Standard protocols<br>• Automated pipeline |
261	
262	
263	### Key Differences:
264	
265	#### 1. AI Integration
266	
267	| Approach | Code Example | Benefits |
268	|----------|--------------|----------|
269	| Traditional | `client = anthropic.Client(api_key)`<br>`response = client.messages.create(...)` | • Complex setup<br>• Custom error handling<br>• Tight coupling |
270	| MCP | `client = SparkMCPClient()`<br>`result = await client.optimize_spark_code(code)` | • Simple interface<br>• Built-in validation<br>• Loose coupling |
271	
272	#### 2. Tool Management
273	
274	| Approach | Code Example | Benefits |
275	|----------|--------------|----------|
276	| Traditional | `class SparkOptimizer:`<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;`def register_tool(self, name, func):`<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;`self.tools[name] = func` | • Manual registration<br>• No validation<br>• Complex maintenance |
277	| MCP | `@register_tool("optimize_spark_code")`<br>`async def optimize_spark_code(code: str):` | • Auto-discovery<br>• Type checking<br>• Easy extension |
278	
279	#### 3. Resource Management
280	
281	| Approach | Code Example | Benefits |
282	|----------|--------------|----------|
283	| Traditional | `def __init__(self):`<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;`self.claude = init_claude()`<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;`self.spark = init_spark()` | • Manual orchestration<br>• Manual cleanup<br>• Error-prone |
284	| MCP | `@requires_resources(["claude_ai", "spark"])`<br>`async def optimize_spark_code(code: str):` | • Auto-coordination<br>• Lifecycle management<br>• Error handling |
285	
286	#### 4. Communication Protocol
287	
288	| Approach | Code Example | Benefits |
289	|----------|--------------|----------|
290	| Traditional | `{"type": "request",`<br>&nbsp;`"payload": {"code": code}}` | • Custom format<br>• Manual validation<br>• Custom debugging |
291	| MCP | `{"method": "tools/call",`<br>&nbsp;`"params": {"name": "optimize_code"}}` | • Standard format<br>• Auto-validation<br>• Easy debugging |
292	
293	## Features
294	
295	- **Intelligent Code Optimization**: Leverages Claude AI to analyze and optimize PySpark code
296	- **Performance Analysis**: Provides detailed analysis of performance differences between original and optimized code
297	- **MCP Architecture**: Implements the Model Context Protocol for standardized AI model interactions
298	- **Easy Integration**: Simple client interface for code optimization requests
299	- **Code Generation**: Automatically saves optimized code to separate files
300	
301	
302	### Advanced Usage
303	
304	You can also use the client programmatically:
305	
306	```python
307	from spark_mcp.client import SparkMCPClient
308	
309	async def main():
310	    # Connect to the MCP server
311	    client = SparkMCPClient()
312	    await client.connect()
313	
314	    # Your Spark code to optimize
315	    spark_code = '''
316	    # Your PySpark code here
317	    '''
318	
319	    # Get optimized code with performance analysis
320	    optimized_code = await client.optimize_spark_code(
321	        code=spark_code,
322	        optimization_level="advanced",
323	        save_to_file=True  # Save to output/optimized_spark_example.py
324	    )
325	    
326	    # Analyze performance differences
327	    analysis = await client.analyze_performance(
328	        original_code=spark_code,
329	        optimized_code=optimized_code,
330	        save_to_file=True  # Save to output/performance_analysis.md
331	    )
332	    
333	    # Run both versions and compare
334	    # You can use the run_optimized.py script or implement your own comparison
335	    
336	    await client.close()
337	    
338	    # Analyze performance
339	    performance = await client.analyze_performance(spark_code, optimized_code)
340	
341	    await client.close()
342	```
343	
344	### Example Input and Output
345	
346	The repository includes an example workflow:
347	
348	1. **Input Code** (`input/spark_code_input.py`):
349	```python
350	# Create DataFrames and join
351	emp_df = spark.createDataFrame(employees, ["id", "name", "age", "dept", "salary"])
352	dept_df = spark.createDataFrame(departments, ["dept", "location", "budget"])
353	
354	# Join and analyze
355	result = emp_df.join(dept_df, "dept") \
356	    .groupBy("dept", "location") \
357	    .agg({"salary": "avg", "age": "avg", "id": "count"}) \
358	    .orderBy("dept")
359	```
360	
361	2. **Optimized Code** (`output/optimized_spark_example.py`):
362	```python
363	# Performance-optimized version with caching and improved configurations
364	spark = SparkSession.builder \
365	    .appName("EmployeeAnalysis") \
366	    .config("spark.sql.shuffle.partitions", 200) \
367	    .getOrCreate()
368	
369	# Create and cache DataFrames
370	emp_df = spark.createDataFrame(employees, ["id", "name", "age", "dept", "salary"]).cache()
371	dept_df = spark.createDataFrame(departments, ["dept", "location", "budget"]).cache()
372	
373	# Optimized join and analysis
374	result = emp_df.join(dept_df, "dept") \
375	    .groupBy("dept", "location") \
376	    .agg(
377	        avg("salary").alias("avg_salary"),
378	        avg("age").alias("avg_age"),
379	        count("id").alias("employee_count")
380	    ) \
381	    .orderBy("dept")
382	```
383	
384	3. **Performance Analysis** (`output/performance_analysis.md`):
385	```markdown
386	## Execution Results Comparison
387	
388	### Timing Comparison
389	- Original Code: 5.18 seconds
390	- Optimized Code: 0.65 seconds
391	- Performance Improvement: 87.4%
392	
393	### Optimization Details
394	- Caching frequently used DataFrames
395	- Optimized shuffle partitions
396	- Improved column expressions
397	- Better memory management
398	```
